# Append (or merge into) data/conf/postfix/extra.cf on the mailcow host.
#
# Postfix main.cf resolves a repeated parameter by taking the LAST
# definition it parses, and mailcow appends extra.cf to the end of the
# generated main.cf. That means we can override smtpd_recipient_restrictions
# here WITHOUT editing mailcow's generated main.cf directly (which gets
# regenerated and would silently drop manual edits).
#
# Steps:
#   1. Get mailcow's current value so nothing else breaks:
#        docker compose exec postfix-mailcow postconf smtpd_recipient_restrictions
#   2. Copy that value below, and insert our check_sasl_access clause FIRST,
#      then check_recipient_access immediately after (so a designated
#      exempt sender is permitted before the suppression check ever runs,
#      and every other sender still gets rejected from unsubscribed
#      recipients before any other rule has a chance to permit/discard them).
#
# Example (adjust to match what step 1 actually returned for your install):

smtpd_recipient_restrictions =
    check_sasl_access mysql:/opt/postfix/conf/sql/mysql-virtual-unsub-exempt-sender.cf,
    check_recipient_access mysql:/opt/postfix/conf/sql/mysql-virtual-unsub-recipient.cf,
    permit_mynetworks,
    permit_sasl_authenticated,
    reject_unauth_destination,
    check_policy_service unix:private/policyd-spf,
    check_policy_service inet:127.0.0.1:10023

# Note: this only exempts specific SASL-authenticated senders (whole
# mailboxes), not individual in-thread replies. Postfix's recipient
# restrictions run at RCPT TO time, before the message body/headers (e.g.
# References/In-Reply-To) even exist on the wire, so "let it through if it's
# a reply" isn't expressible here -- that would require a content_filter or
# milter that inspects headers instead of (or in addition to) this
# check_recipient_access map. Not implemented; use the sender exemption
# above for mailboxes that need to keep corresponding 1:1 with unsubscribed
# addresses (e.g. support@, or a personal mailbox).
